# Build context exclusions, shared by all three service images.
#
# WHY THIS FILE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. Without it the build context is the
# whole repository including `node_modules`, `zebrad-data` and `postgres-data` -
# the last two are bind-mount directories the compose stack creates beside itself
# on the VPS, and a synced Zebra state directory is hundreds of gigabytes. A
# missing `.dockerignore` sends that to the daemon on every build.
#
# It also keeps `.env` out of every image. Nothing in `docker build` needs a
# secret, and an image layer is readable by anyone who can pull it.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* EXCLUDED, and the reason, because getting this
# wrong fails the build in a way that is confusing rather than obvious: every
# workspace `package.json`, including `legacy/dashboard`'s and `apps/web`'s.
# `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` reconciles the lockfile against EVERY importer
# `pnpm-workspace.yaml` resolves, so a manifest missing from the context makes
# the lockfile look out of date and the install fails - even though the image
# builds only one app. The Dockerfiles copy the manifests first and the sources
# after, so the cost of keeping them is one small file each. Excluding a whole
# directory and then trying to re-include a file inside it with `!` is the
# version of this that looks correct and is not.

# Dependencies and build output - reinstalled and rebuilt inside the image. A
# host `node_modules` would be actively wrong here: it is resolved for the host
# platform and this image is linux/amd64 musl.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
.turbo
**/.turbo
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Runtime state written beside the compose file on the VPS. See the volumes in
# docker-compose.yml. These are the entries that matter most for build speed.
zebrad-data
postgres-data
redis-data

# Secrets. `.env.example` carries no values and is documentation, so it stays.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control, CI and editor state.
.git
.github
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea

# Heavy documentation and media. Named individually rather than by excluding
# `docs/` wholesale, so nothing a build needs disappears with them.
docs/2.0/research
docs/2.0/mockups
docs/2.0/screens
docs/2.0/v0.2-notes
docs/2.0/AQUA-STACK-v4.1.png
handoffs

# Test surface. No service image runs a test, and `apps/indexer/test/fixtures`
# alone is most of a megabyte.
**/__tests__
**/*.test.ts
apps/indexer/test
apps/web/test
packages/content/test
packages/zebra-rpc/test
playwright-report
test-results
coverage
**/coverage

# The web app's source. The three service images are the indexer, the gateway
# and the publisher; `apps/web` is built by Vercel, never here. Its manifest is
# kept, per the note above; its source is not.
apps/web/src
apps/web/public
apps/web/scripts

# Local tooling artefacts.
*.log
.DS_Store
